Purpose of the Project 
The main aim of this project is to build a command-line library system in Python. 
It can store books, register students, issue and return books, and calculate late fees on its own.
The project also gave me a chance to use the basic Python topics we studied in class, such as functions, loops, lists, dictionaries and importing code from other files.

Problem Statement Manual handling of library records creates a few problems for institutions:
• It is hard to keep track of stock when many students borrow and return books. 
• A book may be issued to someone who is not registered, or when no copy is left. 
• The same student can end up borrowing the same book twice. 
• Late fees are often calculated wrongly because the number of days is counted by hand.
Without an automated system, record keeping takes long, is full of errors, and is difficult to verify later.


#Functional Requirements 
The program has four main modules, and all of them are used through an 8-option menu that keeps running until the user chooses to exit. 


Module 1: Storage and Data Initialization (storage.py)
Structures-books, users, issued_records
Input -Pre-filled data, or new dictionaries added while the program runs
Processing-Keeps global lists of dictionaries for books, registered users and active loans
Output-Data stored in memory while the program is running
Purpose-Keeps all the shared data of the application in one place


Module 2: Book Catalog Management (books.py)
Functions- list_all_books(), find_book_by_id(book_id), add_new_book(book_id, title, author, quantity)
Input- Book ID, title, author and quantity
Processing- Goes through the books using while loops, checks the ID (spaces are removed and case is ignored in main.py), and adds a new book dictionary
Output- List of books with their stock, or a success/error message
Purpose- Manages the library inventory


Module 3: User Management (users.py)
Functions- find_user_by_id(user_id), register_student(user_id, name), list_all_students()
Input- Student ID and student name
Processing -Checks if the ID already exists, then adds a new student dictionary with "role": "student"
Output-List of registered students, or an error message
Purpose -Keeps the student profiles


Module 4: Borrow, Return and Fine Management (borrow_service.py)
Functions- issue_book(student_id, book_id, days), calculate_fine(days_issued), return_book(student_id, book_id), list_issued_books()
Input -Student ID, book ID and days kept (integer)
Issue- Checks that the student and the book exist, that stock is more than 0, and that the student doesn't already have the same book. Then it reduces the quantity by 1 and saves the loan.
Fine- If days_issued > 14, fine = (days_issued - 14) x 5, otherwise 0
Return -Finds the loan record, calculates the fine, increases the book quantity by 1 and removes the record
Output- Success messages, stock warnings, fine amount (in Rs.) and the list of issued books
Purpose -Handles the whole borrow-return cycle and the overdue rule


#Non-Functional Requirements
• Usability: A simple 8-option menu with direct prompts for input.
• Maintainability: The code is divided into 5 files (storage.py, books.py, users.py, borrow_service.py, main.py) so each file has its own job. 
• Reliability: It stops wrong operations such as issuing an out-of-stock book, issuing to a student who doesn't exist, issuing the same book twice, or returning a book twice.
• Error handling: .isdigit() is used to check numeric inputs (menu choice, quantity, days kept), and .strip().lower() is used to clean IDs before comparing them.
• Performance: It is very light because it only uses basic Python lists and dictionaries.
